<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Jorvik Daily News</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Jorvik Daily News</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>cc.jorviksoftware.JorvikDailyNews</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.1.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>JorvikDailyNews</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>14.0</string>
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- App Transport Security, and why it is set this way.

         NSAllowsArbitraryLoads is ON because a feed reader has to fetch what
         people are subscribed to, and a great deal of RSS is still served over
         plain http. Measured on a real subscription list: 178 of 247 feed URLs
         begin http://. Refusing those would not make the app safer, it would
         make it useless, and the feed itself is treated as hostile input
         regardless of how it arrived.

         NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent WAS here, set to false, and it
         had to be removed. **ATS ignores NSAllowsArbitraryLoads whenever the
         web-content key is PRESENT, whatever value it carries.** Proved with
         two app bundles differing in nothing else, both fetching the same
         http:// feed: the one without the key got a real network answer, the
         one with it got "the App Transport Security policy requires the use
         of a secure connection". So adding it turned cleartext fetching off
         app-wide, and every remaining plain-http feed failed hourly, in
         silence, from the moment it shipped.

         The reasoning behind it was right and is kept — a web view runs the
         page's own scripts, and over cleartext an on-path attacker can
         rewrite that page and run script inside the app's chrome, where there
         is no address bar to check. It is enforced in
         LiveWebView's navigation policy instead, which is the one view that
         loads a remote page, and which already refuses anything WebURL
         refuses. The cost was measured before it was chosen: of 898 stored
         article links, 6 are plain http, roughly one live-page fallback in
         150. -->
    <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>
    <key>SUFeedURL</key>
    <string>https://jorviksoftware.cc/appcasts/jorvikdailynews.xml</string>
    <key>SUPublicEDKey</key>
    <string>zVc7rs8EiLmlDAYpgaIItjnHtsCkvyVVub+I0/sSb3Q=</string>
    <!-- Required for a SANDBOXED app: Sparkle installs through its bundled
         Installer.xpc rather than in-process. Without this an update check
         succeeds and the install silently does nothing. The matching
         mach-lookup names are in JorvikDailyNews.entitlements. -->
    <key>SUEnableInstallerLauncherService</key>
    <true/>
    <key>SUEnableAutomaticChecks</key>
    <true/>
    <key>SUScheduledCheckInterval</key>
    <integer>86400</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
